In article <2201@quiche.cs.mcgill.ca> samurai@quiche.cs.mcgill.ca (Darcy BROCKBANK) writes:
>In article <2290@castle.ed.ac.uk> egnr69@castle.ed.ac.uk (I Chisholm) writes:
>>Why, on level 28, did I wield a cockatrice corpse and then fall down the
>>staircase, accidentally turning to stone!!!!!!!!!
>
>I did this myself once... you may have been carrying near your limit
>( you have a little trouble lifting a cockatrice corpse...).
>
>When you are near your limit, you lose all kinds of co-ordination.
>You fall down stairs, kick clumsily ( or not at all ( you can`t
>balance yourself properly for a kick!)) and if you happen to be
>carrying a cockatrice corpse you will get stoned ! Also if you
>are dragging an iron ball it will hit you causing a fair amount of
>damage. 
>
>Question : Anybody know what the hit factor is for a character near its
>weight limit ?
>

Your carrying-weight has nothing to do with your chances of getting hit by the
iron ball. As of PL6, you have different, interrelated chances of getting hit 
or "dragged down" the stairs when you are being punished. Hitting causes more 
damage, but dragging down can cause you to drop items. The relative chances of 
hitting or dragging depends primarily on how you are transporting the ball, 
plus some other factors. Use your common sense.

As far as cockatrice corpses go, anytime you fall while wielding a cockatrice
corpse, you risk stoning (if you're not polymorphed into a stoning-resistant
monster, of course). It's been that way for a while. If wielding a cockatrice
corpse didn't have some risks involved, it would hurt game balance.

